<v Jonathan Menjivar>It is recording.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>正在录音。

00:42:55.750 --> 00:42:56.260
<v Benjamin>Can you turn it off for us?
<v Benjamin>能为我们关掉吗？

00:42:56.260 --> 00:42:56.930
<v Jonathan Menjivar>Sure. Sure.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>当然。当然。

00:42:56.930 --> 00:42:58.180
<v Benjamin>Come on in. Come.
<v Benjamin>进来吧。进来。

00:42:58.180 --> 00:43:07.080
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Benjamin greeted us at the door to his home and let us in. He's a tall black man, about 40. He was in socks and a sweater. The place was modest.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>本杰明在门口迎接我们并让我们进去。他是一个高个子黑人，大约40岁。他穿着袜子和毛衣。房间很简朴。

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:25.990
<v Scott Pinkmountain>We entered the living room, and the woman who'd answered the door earlier, who turned out to be Benjamin's wife, was in one corner. A boy who was introduced to us as their younger son was on the couch playing a video game. There was no sign of Anthony. But I also didn't feel comfortable asking where he was right off the bat.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们进入客厅，早先开门的那个女人（原来是本杰明的妻子）在角落里。一个被介绍给我们说是他们小儿子的男孩在沙发上玩游戏。没有安东尼的踪影。但我也不觉得舒服一上来就问他在哪。

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:37.350
<v Scott Pinkmountain>We thought maybe the house would be packed with drawings and paintings by artists that Benjamin had written to. But there was no art on the walls. Instead, there were nails and hooks where things appeared to usually hang.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们原以为房子里会塞满本杰明写信给的艺术家的素描和画作。但墙上没有艺术品。相反，墙上有钉子和钩子，看起来通常会挂东西。

00:43:37.350 --> 00:44:01.570
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Without any prompting, Benjamin explained they were in the middle of renovation, so they took everything down. There was tape around the edge of some of the windows, and the room smelled faintly of paint. We sat down, and Benjamin asked a lot of questions, what our motivation was, who this was for. Were we going to edit and manipulate the tape? Then he said OK, and then we started. I asked him to explain what's going on.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>没有受到任何提示，本杰明解释说他们正在装修，所以他们把一切都取下了。窗户边缘有一些胶带，房间里隐约闻到油漆味。我们坐下来，本杰明问了很多问题：我们的动机是什么，这是给谁做的。我们会编辑和篡改录音吗？然后他说好，我们就开始了。我请他解释一下发生了什么。

00:44:01.570 --> 00:44:20.910
<v Benjamin>Well, you know, from my perspective, Anthony is autistic. And he's got a gift, in my opinion, for art. Now, regarding contact with other artists, from my understanding, there's a tradition of artists exchanging works.
<v Benjamin>嗯，你知道，从我的角度来看，安东尼是自闭症。他有天赋，在我看来，艺术天赋。至于与其他艺术家的联系，据我理解，有一种艺术家交流作品的传统。

00:44:20.910 --> 00:44:32.690
<v Benjamin>They exchange works left, right, and center. Internationally, there's this constant swapping of art, you know? So we're not doing anything different from what they're doing amongst themselves.
<v Benjamin>他们一直在交流作品。在国际上，有一种持续的艺术交换，你知道吗？所以我们做的与他们自己在做的事情没什么不同。

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:54.780
<v Benjamin>It's just that, probably, we're breaking into their circle in an unconventional manner. Now, who's this dude? He's written to that person and that person and that, but who is he? Is he real? Is he some fiction? Is he some big scam? And he's real.
<v Benjamin>只是，可能，我们是以非常规的方式进入了他们的圈子。现在，这个家伙是谁？他写信给那个人、那个人，但他是谁？他是真的吗？他是一些虚构吗？他是一些大骗局吗？他是真的。

00:44:54.780 --> 00:44:58.800
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Yeah, I mean what would you say to that? Is Anthony real?
<v Scott Pinkmountain>是的，我是说你会对那个说什么？安东尼是真的吗？

00:44:58.800 --> 00:45:08.190
<v Benjamin>Of course, Anthony's real. Yeah, he's real. Yeah, he's absolutely real.
<v Benjamin>当然，安东尼是真的。是的，他是真的。是的，他是绝对真实的。

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:22.600
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Benjamin said, you'll get to see him. And we realized, oh, he's actually here in the house somewhere, though Benjamin didn't seem to be in a hurry to go get him. And before we had a chance to ask, Benjamin made it clear he wasn't going to let us speak to Anthony.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>本杰明说：你会见到他的。我们意识到，噢，他实际上就在这房子里的某个地方，尽管本杰明似乎并不急着去把他带过来。在我们有机会询问之前，本杰明明确表示他不会让我们和安东尼说话。

00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:42.060
<v Scott Pinkmountain>We wouldn't be able to talk to him directly about these art trades. Benjamin later explained Anthony has trouble in social situations and especially with communication. He said it was best to speak on his son's behalf. We asked Benjamin how many artists he'd written to the way he'd written to Schandra and Baker. He wouldn't say.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们将无法直接和他谈论这些艺术交换。本杰明后来解释说安东尼在社交场合有困难，特别是在沟通方面。他说最好代他的儿子说话。我们问本杰明他写信给多少艺术家，方式和写给桑德拉和贝克的一样。他没说。

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:47.120
<v Scott Pinkmountain>How many artists have you contacted or communicated with about this?
<v Scott Pinkmountain>你联系或交流过的艺术家有多少？

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:50.480
<v Benjamin>How many artists have spoken to you about it?
<v Benjamin>有多少艺术家和你谈论过它？

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:51.700
<v Scott Pinkmountain>A couple.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>几个。

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:58.220
<v Benjamin>Well, off the top of my head, I don't know. But let's just leave it at that.
<v Benjamin>嗯，我想不起来。但让我们就留在那儿吧。

00:45:58.220 --> 00:45:59.610
<v Scott Pinkmountain>I mean, I guess--
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我是说，我想——

00:45:59.610 --> 00:46:11.330
<v Benjamin>Specific numbers, I don't know specific numbers. Well, we'll put it this way. There have been quite a few. Put it that way. There have been quite a few. But to give you a number off the top of my head, no. But there have been quite a few.
<v Benjamin>具体数字，我不知道具体数字。嗯，我们换个说法。已经有不少了。就这么说吧。已经有不少了。但要让我直接给你一个数字，不。但已经有不少了。

00:46:11.330 --> 00:46:15.640
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Have quite a few responded by giving you drawings or paintings?
<v Scott Pinkmountain>有不少人通过给你素描或画作来回应吗？

