<v Benjamin>Well, I'm guessing if you guys flew in from New York, this is not just one or two drawings we're talking about. If you guys have come all the way from New York for this-- well, there's been quite a few. It's been quite a few. Put it that way.
<v Benjamin>嗯，我猜如果你们从纽约飞过来，我们谈论的不只是一两幅素描。如果你们为了这个从纽约大老远过来——嗯，已经有不少了。这已经不少了。就这么说吧。

00:46:28.390 --> 00:46:33.850
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Are we talking like 10 or 100 or--
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们是在谈论像10还是100或者——

00:46:33.850 --> 00:46:37.060
<v Baker Overstreet>Well, quite a few is what I will say.
<v Baker Overstreet>嗯，有不少是我会说的。

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:43.310
<v Scott Pinkmountain>We were annoying. We were persistent. At one point, Benjamin looked around the living room and answered at least a little more fully.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们很烦人。我们很执着。有一次，本杰明环顾客厅，回答得稍微完整了一点。

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:51.830
<v Benjamin>If you take a look behind you, unfortunately, it was a blank wall. No, it's a blank wall. But you'll see there's some nails that we've put in. And it's because we've been painting.
<v Benjamin>如果你看看你身后，不幸的是，那是一面空白的墙。不，它是一面空白的墙。但你会看到有一些钉子是我们钉上去的。那是因为我们一直在刷漆。

00:46:51.830 --> 00:47:05.360
<v Benjamin>And they're right up there, basically. Can you see? Can you acknowledge that you do see? You do see them. So, basically, we hang them up on the wall. I mean, over here, that's  one work. That's another work.
<v Benjamin>它们就在上面，基本上。你能看到吗？你能承认你确实看到了吗？你确实看到了它们。所以，基本上，我们把它们挂在墙上。我是说，在这儿，那是一件作品。那是另一件作品。

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:06.820
<v Scott Pinkmountain>So we're looking at a blank wall here.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>所以我们在这儿看一面空白的墙。

00:47:06.820 --> 00:47:15.270
<v Benjamin>That's another work. It's a blank wall because we're painting. That's another work. That's another work. That's another one. That's another one. That's another one.
<v Benjamin>那是另一件作品。那是一面空白的墙，因为我们在刷漆。那是另一件作品。那是另一件作品。那是另一件。那是另一件。那是另一件。

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:26.880
<v Scott Pinkmountain>There were maybe a dozen blank spots on the wall. Benjamin confirmed their collection was larger than that and added that they also change up what's hanging so the kids can appreciate different works. We asked for the names of the artists.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>墙上可能有十几个空白处。本杰明证实了他们的收藏比那个还要多，并补充说他们也更换挂什么东西，这样孩子们可以欣赏不同的作品。我们询问艺术家的名字。

00:47:26.880 --> 00:47:41.970
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Benjamin either said he couldn't remember off the top of his head or he didn't want to violate their privacy. We asked to see Schandra's drawing. We wondered if he'd sold it. He went upstairs and was gone for a few minutes. Then he came back with the drawing. It was unframed.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>本杰明要么说他想不起来，要么说他不想违反他们的隐私。我们要求看桑德拉的素描。我们纳闷他是否卖了。他上楼去了几分钟。然后他带着那幅素描回来了。它没有装框。

00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:53.100
<v Benjamin>Yeah, so this is it. We're going to have it framed and put it up eventually.
<v Benjamin>是的，所以就是这个。我们最终会把它装框挂起来。

00:47:53.100 --> 00:48:03.320
<v Scott Pinkmountain>We asked lots of other questions. Why was Benjamin's correspondence with different artists almost word for word the same? He said, it's just a formality. Why vary it?
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们问了很多其他问题。为什么你和不同艺术家的信件几乎一字不差地一样？他说：这只是一个形式。为什么要改变它？

00:48:03.320 --> 00:48:15.790
<v Scott Pinkmountain>We asked, how did Anthony find out about Schandra's work? Benjamin explained that when Anthony finds artists, well, obviously, there's some adult input. He said, I can't recall where we came across her wonderful work.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们问：安东尼是怎么发现桑德拉的作品的？本杰明解释说，当安东尼找到艺术家时，嗯，显然，会有一些成年人的投入。他说：我想不起来我们在哪里偶然发现了她精彩的作品。

00:48:15.790 --> 00:48:28.720
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Did you learn about Schandra and Baker's work from the Saatchi website? He said he found Schandra's work online but wouldn't get more specific. Did Anthony really have a special response to Schandra's work? How do you know what he responds to?
<v Scott Pinkmountain>你是从萨奇网站上了解到桑德拉和贝克的作品的吗？他说他在网上找到了桑德拉的作品，但拒绝透露细节。安东尼真的对桑德拉的作品有特别的反应吗？你怎么知道他有什么反应？

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:44.170
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Benjamin basically said a parent can just tell. Have you sold any of the work you've received? He replied that 99.99% of the collection is intact. After almost an hour of talking, my producer Jonathan cut in with this question.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>本杰明基本上说父母一眼就能看出来。你卖掉过你收到的作品吗？他回答说99.9%的收藏品都在。交谈近一小时后，我的制片人乔纳森插话问了这个问题。

00:48:44.170 --> 00:48:58.470
<v Jonathan Menjivar>One of the possibilities, when you look at the situation, is that you're using his autism to collect art, that it is some version of a scam.
<v Jonathan Menjivar>当你看这种情况时，可能性之一是你在利用他的自闭症来收藏艺术品，这是一种骗局。

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:27.920
<v Benjamin>That's interesting you say. I mean, yeah, you could say that. That's one way of looking at it, absolutely. I mean, it's not a scam. Put it that way. And I would understand if people thought that. But it isn't a scam. It's just a simple love of art, you know? That's all it is. Yeah.
<v Benjamin>这很有意思。我是说，是的，你可以那样说。那绝对是一种看待它的方式。我是说，这不是骗局。就这么说吧。如果人们那样认为，我也会理解。但它不是骗局。这只是对艺术的简单热爱，你知道吗？这就是全部。是的。

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:37.220
<v Scott Pinkmountain>At one point, Benjamin was explaining to us what Anthony's early drawings were like. He told us Anthony was obsessed with trains. And then Anthony just wandered into the room.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>有一次，本杰明正在向我们解释安东尼早期的素描是什么样的。他告诉我们安东尼迷上了火车。然后安东尼刚好走进房间。

00:49:37.220 --> 00:49:41.480
<v Benjamin>--trains to begin with. That's Anthony by the way.
<v Benjamin>——最初迷上火车。顺便说一下，这是安东尼。

00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:50.280
<v Scott Pinkmountain>Here's one question we got a definitive answer to. Anthony is very real. He's a tall, broad shouldered teenager. He's 15 now.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我们得到了一个明确答案的问题是：安东尼是真实存在的。他是一个高大、肩膀宽阔的少年。他现在15岁。

00:49:50.280 --> 00:50:02.720
<v Scott Pinkmountain>He was barefoot, and I could see that a few of his toes and fingers were slightly malformed. He only kind of acknowledged us and went and sat beside his mother. I don't personally know much about autism, but Anthony appeared to have a disorder.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>他是赤脚的，我能看到他的几个脚趾和手指有点畸形。他只是某种程度上承认了我们，然后走到他母亲身边坐下。我个人对自闭症了解不多，但安东尼看起来确实有某种障碍。

00:50:02.720 --> 00:50:18.580
<v Scott Pinkmountain>He spoke a few times but never to us directly. His speech was slow and hard to understand. Benjamin told us that their other son is also autistic. Benjamin mentioned that Anthony played piano. And when we finished our interview, they brought down Anthony's keyboard and set it on the table.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>他说了几次话，但从没直接对我们说。他的说话很慢，很难理解。本杰明告诉我们，他们的另一个儿子也是自闭症。本杰明提到安东尼弹钢琴。当我们采访结束时，他们拿下了安东尼的键盘，放在桌子上。

00:50:18.580 --> 00:50:21.650
<v Anthony>I'm going to play rap.
<v Anthony>我要弹说唱。

00:50:21.650 --> 00:50:24.830
<v Benjamin>You want to play rap?
<v Benjamin>你想弹说唱？

00:50:24.830 --> 00:50:36.280
<v Benjamin>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Benjamin>[播放音乐]

00:50:36.280 --> 00:50:54.830
<v Scott Pinkmountain>I'm a musician myself, and I've heard hundreds of piano improvisations. Anthony's music had the kind of spaciousness, repetition, and tiny deviations of some of my favorite avant-garde music. It was both thoughtful and intuitive, strangely confident and vulnerable at the same time. I was hearing something unique.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我自己就是音乐家，我听过数百次钢琴即兴演奏。安东尼的音乐具有我最喜爱的一些前卫音乐那种空间感、重复性和微小的偏差。它既体贴又直观，同时又奇怪地自信而脆弱。我听到了一些独特的东西。

00:50:54.830 --> 00:51:05.320
<v Scott Pinkmountain>I was really conflicted. I liked the family. They'd let us into their home and offered as tea and tolerated our questions. I believed that these were two parents who care deeply for their children.
<v Scott Pinkmountain>我真的很矛盾。我喜欢这家人。他们让我们进了他们的家，为我们提供茶，并忍受了我们的问题。我相信这是两位深深关爱孩子的父母。

